 "top": [[" is", -0.0010699391132220626], [" parallel", -7.637195587158203]]}, {"t": " generally", "lp": -1.026497483253479, "top": [[" generally", -1.026497483253479], [" significantly", -1.5719038248062134]]}, {"t": " faster", "lp": -0.01498982310295105, "top": [[" faster", -0.01498982310295105], [" much", -4.387639999389648]]}, {"t": " and", "lp": -0.6868882775306702, "top": [[" and", -0.6868882775306702], [" for", -1.060564637184143]]}, {"t": " better", "lp": -0.616201639175415, "top": [[" better", -0.616201639175415], [" more", -1.9208511114120483]]}, {"t": " at", "lp": -0.10483536124229431, "top": [[" at", -0.10483536124229431], [" for", -2.9934728145599365]]}, {"t": " parallel", "lp": -0.3753817081451416, "top": [[" parallel", -0.3753817081451416], [" handling", -1.3006538152694702]]}], "text": "Here is a step-by-step breakdown of the `build()` function in your PKGBUILD.\n\n### 1. Environment Setup\n```bash\nsource /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh --force\n```\n*   **Purpose:** This script initializes the Intel oneAPI environment. It sets environment variables like `INTEL_ONEAPI_ROOT`, `LIBRARY_PATH`, and `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` so that the compiler (`icpx`) and linker can find Intel's libraries (MKL, oneDNN, Level Zero, etc.).\n*   **`--force`:** This flag forces the script to overwrite existing environment variables, ensuring a clean slate for the build environment.\n\n### 2. Error Handling\n```bash\nset -e\n```\n*   **Purpose:** This is a standard Bash safety measure. It tells the shell to **exit immediately** if any command returns a non-zero exit code (an error).\n*   **Why it's here:** Without this, if `cmake` fails, the script might continue trying to run `ninja` or other commands, leading to confusing errors or incomplete builds.\n\n### 3. CMake Configuration\n```bash\ncmake -S \"${srcdir}/llama.cpp\" -B \"${srcdir}/build\" -G Ninja \\\n```\n*   **`-S \"${srcdir}/llama.cpp\"`:** Specifies the **source directory**. This is where the downloaded git repository lives.\n*   **`-B \"${srcdir}/build\"`:** Specifies the **build directory**. CMake will generate all intermediate files (Makefiles, Ninja build files, object files) here. This keeps the source tree clean.\n*   **`-G Ninja`:** Tells CMake to generate build files for **Ninja** instead of Make. Ninja is generally faster and better at parallel"}